  "body": "The maintainer reported this in chat on 2026-11-16. His words: \"its just to much compile in each \"batch\".. so it takes forever to get thru a post-merge-gate - even with -j6 set.. if u set a -j6.. there should be 2 cores per procss. so a max of 3 tests per batch..\"\n\n## What happens\n\n- `xtask/src/plan.rs` gives each cargo step `-j 6` and `--test-threads=6` (`J`, `T`).\n- `xtask/src/lanes.rs` runs up to `LIMIT = 4` steps at the same time in the release tier.\n- Each lane keeps the full `-j 6` and `--test-threads=6`. Four lanes thus start up to 24 compile jobs and 24 test threads.\n- The gate also shares its machine with review builds and worker builds. Each of those uses `-j 6` too.\n- The result is more jobs than cores. Each lane is slower than a serial run.\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Make the job count one budget for the whole gate (default 6). Read it from one constant, with an environment override.\n2. When N lanes run at the same time, give each lane `-j (budget / N)` and `--test-threads=(budget / N)`, minimum 1.\n3. Set the lane limit from the budget: at most `budget / 2` lanes. With a budget of 6 this is 3 lanes with 2 jobs each.\n4. A step that runs alone keeps the full budget.\n5. Print the budget, the lane count and the jobs for each lane at the start of the gate.\n6. Apply the same rule to each tier that runs lanes, and to #1941 if it adds lanes to the quick and batch tiers.\n7. Update the `gate` skill and the `runners` skill: the sum of `-j` of all builds on one machine stays at or below its core count.\n\n## Done means\n\n- A unit test in `xtask` shows that the sum of `-j` in each wave is at or below the budget, and that no wave has more than `budget / 2` lanes.\n- A unit test shows that a step that runs alone gets the full budget.\n- `cargo xtask gate release --dry-run` prints the budget and the jobs for each lane.\n- The PR body has the wall time of one batch run and one release wave, before and after.\n- The PR body has the line `Process change: yes`.\n",
